WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.889
Do you guys remember the Tesla roadster?

00:00:02.889 --> 00:00:07.050
Not the classic version but the new one that
Elon announced in 2017.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:14.009
The new roadster was supposed to have it all,
a 1.9 second 0 to 60, a top speed of 250 mph,

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:16.350
and a range of 620 miles.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:21.180
Coming it at a starting price of $200,000,
the first roadsters were expected to be delivered

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.730
in 2020.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.090
But clearly, that didn’t happen.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:29.600
And now, it seems like roadsters won’t be
hitting the road till at least 2023.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.789
So, what in the world happened to the Tesla
roadster?

00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:39.489
Taking a look at the timeline of Roadster
delays, it’s actually not too exciting.

00:00:39.489 --> 00:00:43.969
The timeline mostly consists of Tesla not
giving any updates and then delaying the car

00:00:43.969 --> 00:00:45.940
right before it was supposed to come out.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:50.679
The most recent update we got was in January
of 2021 when Elon tweeted that engineering

00:00:50.679 --> 00:00:55.729
would be finished by the end of 2021, and
that production would begin in 2022.

00:00:55.729 --> 00:00:59.410
Given that roadster prototypes apparently
hit all of their promised numbers years ago,

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.170
what’s the big hold up?

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:05.390
Well, likely the biggest obstacle that they
didn’t expect to face when announcing the

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.670
roadster was a near death experience.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:11.340
Around the time the roadster was initially
announced, the model 3 was finally hitting

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.340
the market.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:17.950
In July of 2017, the first 30 model 3 production
vehicles were delivered, and Tesla had a small

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.280
celebration event.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.119
Ironically though, there wasn’t too much
to celebrate as Elon as well as the Tesla

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:28.850
employees knew that ramping up production
of the model 3 was going to be a nightmare.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:34.100
You see, the first 30 model 3 vehicles were
mostly built by hand and Tesla was yet to

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.740
employ their massive automated assembly line.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:41.479
Tesla knew that ramping up automated production
was going to be rather challenging, but they

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:44.250
didn’t expect it to nearly wipe them out.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.860
When Tesla started production, issues would
spring up instantly.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:52.369
The robots kept misaligning parts of the car,
the software kept crashing, and the worst

00:01:52.369 --> 00:01:55.430
part was that no one really knew why.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:00.070
Tesla had spent over a billion dollars building
the Gigafactory, but it looked like the factory

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:01.750
was a lemon.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:05.810
Hundreds of quote on quote under performing
employees would be fired and the remaining

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.640
ones would be pushed extremely hard to meet
production numbers.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.069
Even with this intense pressure though, Tesla
was nowhere near their 5000 cars a week production

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:14.099
goal.

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:18.390
Every week, Tesla didn’t meet production
numbers, they were burning loads of money,

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.180
and at one point, Tesla was within single
digit weeks of dying.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:27.129
The company would eventually figure it out,
and the answer was to simply use less automation.

00:02:27.129 --> 00:02:31.210
But reaching production numbers was just the
first of their worries.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:35.290
Once the cars were manufactured, they actually
had to be delivered, and the problem was that

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.610
each delivery was built to order.

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:41.940
Generally, when you buy a car, you just go
to the closest dealer and pick out a car from

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.049
their lot.

00:02:43.049 --> 00:02:46.940
With Tesla though, everyone custom builds
their cars online.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:51.260
There are only a limited number of options,
but given the relatively low volume of cars

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:55.239
Tesla was building, it was especially hard
to come up with an efficient delivery plan

00:02:55.239 --> 00:02:57.880
which led them to losing even more money.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:02.220
Considering this massive debacle Tesla experienced,
it’s not hard to see why the roadster would

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.180
be the last thing on their list.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.629
It should be noted that even if Tesla didn’t
experience the model 3 production nightmare,

00:03:08.629 --> 00:03:11.379
the roadster would still be last priority.

00:03:11.379 --> 00:03:15.850
Here’s the thing, the roadster targets an
extremely niche market and it's unlikely that

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:20.500
Tesla will crack the six figures and maybe
even the 5 figures in terms of sales.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:25.020
So, it makes little sense for them to devote
much effort into the roadster.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.860
This is especially true when you consider
that Tesla still has lots of room to grow

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.629
within their fundamental business itself.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:36.439
Just last year, they started production of
the Model Y and looking forward, they want

00:03:36.439 --> 00:03:39.510
to start cyber truck production by the end
of this year.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:43.410
And the Austin Gigafactory where the cybertruck
is supposed to be produced is still under

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:44.770
construction itself.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:48.930
It’s also likely that Tesla would prefer
to avoid a production nightmare when it comes

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.510
to the cybertruck and their new gigafactories.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:56.090
Evidently, there’s plenty of things to focus
on in their primary business itself.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:01.870
So, the roadster has never been a priority
and likely won't be one moving forward either.

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:06.440
The roadster is not only a low priority, but
it also comes along with a massive price tag

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.940
in terms of research and development.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:14.719
Just think about it, the roadster is supposed
to have a sub 2 0 to 60, a 620 mile range,

00:04:14.719 --> 00:04:17.630
and it’s of course supposed to be fully
electric.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:22.340
Developing something like this would no doubt
cost tens if not hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:27.080
For a company like Tesla that's really just
breaking even at this point, such an expense

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.900
is not negligible by any means.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:34.170
Moreover, this money could be spent in other
sectors which would be much more beneficial

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:35.580
for Tesla.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:39.340
For instance, they could use the money to
make the austin gigafactory bigger and produce

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:40.750
more cars.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:44.400
Or they could use the money for self driving
R&amp;D which could accelerate Tesla’s vision

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.710
of creating robotaxis.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:49.539
Now, money spent on roadster R&amp;D isn’t a
waste per se.

00:04:49.539 --> 00:04:54.169
It’s common to develop new technology on
flagship experimental vehicles, and then slowly

00:04:54.169 --> 00:04:56.449
bring down that technology to lower tiers
of vehicles.

00:04:56.449 --> 00:05:01.520
With the roadster, Tesla has to develop more
powerful motors and higher density batteries,

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.310
and these technologies could definitely be
brought down to the model s and x and even

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.010
the 3 and y eventually.

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:12.000
But, though having a model 3 with a range
of 1000 miles or something like that would

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:17.310
be insane, would tripling the range of a model
3 really triple the sales of the model 3.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:18.310
Not really.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:22.680
So, Tesla isn’t really in any rush to develop
this advanced technology and bring it down

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.430
to their lower models.

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:28.139
But wait a minute, didn’t Tesla reveal that
they already have working prototypes that

00:05:28.139 --> 00:05:30.150
can hit all of the specs they outlined?

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:33.190
In that case, most of the R&amp;D is already completed
right?

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:39.539
Well, that only indicates that Tesla has completed
tech based R&amp;D not cost based R&amp;D.

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:43.740
Not only is Tesla promising specs that destroy
Bugattis, but Tesla is promising to slash

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.699
the price over 10 fold.

00:05:45.699 --> 00:05:49.259
It’s very possible that Tesla figured out
how to achieve the Roadster’s specs years

00:05:49.259 --> 00:05:54.169
ago, but the problem is that it cost $5 or
$6 million to produce.

00:05:54.169 --> 00:05:58.919
With scale, maybe they can bring it down to
$1 to $2 million, but that’s still a far

00:05:58.919 --> 00:06:01.539
ways off from $200,000 a car.

00:06:01.539 --> 00:06:06.080
So, the engineering challenge holding back
Tesla may very well be a cost based one as

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.030
opposed to a tech based one.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:12.319
Until now, we’ve discussed a bunch of financial
and technical challenges in bringing the roadster

00:06:12.319 --> 00:06:17.570
to market, but I don’t think the main reason
has anything to do with objective obstacles.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:22.070
Rather, I think the main reason the roadster
has yet to hit the roads is because it's a

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.220
marketing tool more than anything.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:29.860
The roadster isn’t built to generate profit
or reduce costs or advance Tesla’s technology.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:35.729
No, the roadster is built for one reason and
one reason only which is building clout for

00:06:35.729 --> 00:06:36.729
Tesla.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:40.380
Since the original roadster, Tesla has focused
on growing organic interest by showcasing

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:42.770
how electric cars could be cool.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:47.949
In the early 2000s, electric cars were seen
as slow, boring, and unreliable.

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:51.350
Tesla created the original roadster to disprove
that notion.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:55.509
Using the profits generated by the sports
car, Tesla created the luxury model s and

00:06:55.509 --> 00:06:56.509
x.

00:06:56.509 --> 00:07:00.590
And using the profits generated by the luxury
models, Tesla created everyday people models

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:02.639
which are the 3 and y.

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:07.090
But at this point, there’s no longer a need
to generate hype for electric vehicles and

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:08.590
especially for Teslas.

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:12.930
Right now, Tesla is facing a supply problem
not a demand problem as they have way more

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.020
demand than they can handle.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:16.800
Tesla didn’t exactly expect this.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:22.310
Sure, they expected the model 3 and y to sell
better than any previous model, but the massive

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:27.500
influx of hype following the announcement
of the Cybertruck throughout 2020 was unprecedented.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:31.139
Tesla no longer needed an extreme sports car
that showed that electric cars were cool.

00:07:31.139 --> 00:07:36.000
The truth was that most Tesla owners didn’t
actually buy a Tesla because it's electric.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:39.650
They actually bought a Tesla because it carries
a certain social status and it had a super

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.540
cool feature which is of course self driving.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.530
The electric aspect of Teslas were simply
a side benefit that made tesla owners feel

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.270
better about themselves.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:52.300
More times than not though, the electric aspect
of teslas were not what pushed customers to

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.199
actually buy a Tesla.

00:07:54.199 --> 00:07:57.210
Basically everyone understands that electric
cars are the future.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:02.069
Sure, there’s some disagreement on when
EVs will take over, but nearly everyone agrees

00:08:02.069 --> 00:08:03.449
that it's inevitable.

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:08.040
So, at this point, Tesla’s primary marketing
goal is no longer to convince people to go

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.250
electric, but rather to go Tesla.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:14.370
And the best way to accomplish this is to
build off of their strengths which is their

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.979
brand image and their autonomous features.

00:08:16.979 --> 00:08:20.729
Considering this shift in market sentiment,
the roadster no longer plays a critical marketing

00:08:20.729 --> 00:08:24.250
role in convincing customers to go electric
and buy a tesla.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:28.139
And so, there’s no rush in bringing the
Roadster to market.

00:08:28.139 --> 00:08:30.250
When will the roadster actually come out then?

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.960
Well, in the 2020 q2 earnings call, Elon Musk
suggested that the roadster would be built

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:37.600
in the Fremont factory in California.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:42.170
The Fremont factory is Tesla’s primary production
plant right now though, so I don’t think

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:46.040
Tesla will want to devote Fremont’s production
capacity to the Roadster till their total

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:48.510
total production output is much higher.

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:52.900
Fortunately, the upcoming gigafactories in
Berlin and Austin are expected to be completed

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:54.880
by the end of 2021.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:58.480
If this is the case, they should be able to
ramp up production throughout the first half

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:02.970
of 2022 which would heavily alleviate the
pressure on the Fremont factory.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:07.020
And at that point, Tesla could start producing
the roadster at the Fremont factory which

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:10.580
would mean that the first deliveries would
take place in the second half of 2022.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:14.830
There’s almost always delays though, so
a more conservative estimate would be the

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:16.400
first half of 2023.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:20.860
So, there’s still about 2 years till we
see roadsters being delivered, but it does

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:23.280
seem like it’s finally going to happen.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.560
Why do you guys think the roadster has been
pushed back so much?

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:27.930
Comment that down below.

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:31.910
Also, drop a like if you’re excited to see
the roadster finally come out.

